### Step 4: Verbatim Low-Level Hardware Map (`config.txt`) [639, 640, 641, 642, 643]

The `/boot/config.txt` file acts as the BIOS configuration for your Raspberry Pi [639]. DietPi configures this file by default to maximize your hardware resource availability, stripping down background overheads like unnecessary GPU memory allocations or unused card-reader interrupts [642, 643].

Below is the **complete, verbatim stock template** of `config.txt` mapped directly from the boot partition [639]:

```ini
### Docs: https://www.raspberrypi.com/documentation/computers/config_txt.html
### Overlays: https://github.com/raspberrypi/firmware/blob/master/boot/overlays/README
#-------Display---------
### If you get no picture, set the following to "1" to apply most compatible HDMI settings.
#hdmi_safe=1
### Uncomment to adjust the HDMI signal strength if you have interferences, blanking, or no display.
### - Ranges from "0" to "11", use values above "7" only if required, e.g. with very long HDMI cable.
### - Defaults to "2" on first RPi1 A/B, else "5". From RPi4 on, this setting is ignored.
#config_hdmi_boost=5
### Uncomment if HDMI display is not detected and composite is being outputted.
#hdmi_force_hotplug=1
### Uncomment to disable HDMI even if plugged, e.g. to force composite output.
#hdmi_ignore_hotplug=1
### Uncomment to enable SDTV/composite output from RPi4 on. This has no effect on previous RPi models.
#enable_tvout=1
### Uncomment to force an HDMI mode rather than DVI. This enables HDMI audio in DMT modes.
#hdmi_drive=2
### Set "hdmi_blanking=1" to allow the display going into standby after 10 minutes without input.
### With default value "0", the display shows a blank screen instead, but will not go into standby.
hdmi_blanking=1
### Set to "1" if your display has a black border of unused pixels visible.
disable_overscan=1
### Uncomment the following to adjust overscan.
### Use positive numbers if console goes off screen, and negative if there is too much border.
#overscan_left=16
#overscan_right=16
#overscan_top=16
#overscan_bottom=16
### LCD/OLED Rotation
#lcd_rotate=0
### Enable KMS/DRM display driver, recommended when a GUI application or desktop is used.
### Remove ",noaudio" if HDMI audio is needed, or enable it via dietpi-config.
#dtoverlay=vc4-kms-v3d,noaudio
#-------RPi camera module-------
#camera_auto_detect=1
#disable_camera_led=1
#-------GPU memory splits-------
gpu_mem_256=16
gpu_mem_512=16
gpu_mem_1024=16
#-------Boot splash screen------
disable_splash=1
#-------Onboard sound-----------
dtparam=audio=off
#-------I2C-------------
#dtparam=i2c_arm=off
#dtparam=i2c_arm_baudrate=100000
#-------SPI-------------
#dtparam=spi=off
#-------Serial/UART-----
### NB: "enable_uart=1" will enforce "core_freq=250" on RPi models with onboard WiFi.
enable_uart=1
#-------SD card HPD-----
### Comment to enable SD card hot-plug detection, while booting via USB or network.
### NB: This causes constant CPU load and kernel errors when no SD card is inserted.
dtparam=sd_poll_once
#-------Overclock-------
temp_limit=65
initial_turbo=20
#over_voltage=0
#arm_freq=900
#core_freq=250
#sdram_freq=400
#over_voltage_min=0
#arm_freq_min=300
#core_freq_min=250
#sdram_freq_min=400
arm_64bit=1
```

---

### Step 5: Verbatim Core Kernel Boot Arguments (`cmdline.txt`) [208]

The `/boot/cmdline.txt` file contains a single, non-line-wrapped sequence of core parameters passed to the Linux kernel at the very early stages of system initialization [208].

Below is the **verbatim stock layout** of your `cmdline.txt` file [208]:

```text
root=PARTUUID=your-card-uuid rootfstype=ext4 rootwait fsck.repair=yes net.ifnames=0 logo.nologo console=serial0,115200 console=tty1
```

> ⚠️ **CRITICAL WARNING**: Do **NOT** copy the `PARTUUID` from this guide verbatim! Each freshly flashed SD card or SSD has its own unique partition UUID. If you copy a different `PARTUUID` verbatim, your Pi will fail to boot with the error `sh: can't access tty`. 
>
> **How to configure this safely**:
> 1. **Leave your stock `cmdline.txt` completely untouched**—it already contains your card's correct, unique `PARTUUID`.
> 2. Alternatively, if you lost your original stock file, you can bypass PARTUUID matching entirely by mapping the root device to the generic SD card block path `/dev/mmcblk0p2` [231]:
>    ```text
>    root=/dev/mmcblk0p2 rootfstype=ext4 rootwait fsck.repair=yes net.ifnames=0 logo.nologo console=serial0,115200 console=tty1
>    ```

#### Key Architecture and Network Explanations:
1. **`net.ifnames=0`**: Disables predictable network interface naming. This maps your network interfaces natively back to classic labels: **`eth0`** for your physical Ethernet card and **`wlan0`** for your Wi-Fi interface. This ensures full synchronicity with pre-configured Docker-compose configs, bridge configurations, and DietPi's internal network stats reporting modules which expect standard naming.
2. **`console=serial0,115200`**: Directs early system console logs to the serial UART pins on your Pi on boot. As this is a headless homelab, you can disable this natively in `dietpi.txt` by setting `CONFIG_SERIAL_CONSOLE_ENABLE=0`, allowing you to free up physical GPIO RX/TX pins for home-automation hardware or sensors without the system terminal hijacking the ports.

---

### Step 6: Hardware-Kernel Verification & Optimization Map (`config-6.18.34+rpt-rpi-v8.txt`) [209]

To verify that your system is operating at peak physical capacity, we have parsed your exact hardware kernel configuration file (`config-6.18.34+rpt-rpi-v8.txt`) extracted directly from your target Raspberry Pi 4 board [209]. This kernel configuration acts as the ultimate truth for how Docker, Portainer, and your software stacks will execute [209].

#### 1. Architecture Alignment & deprecation Protection
* **Kernel Flags**: `CONFIG_ARM64=y`, `CONFIG_64BIT=y` [220].
* **Optimization Match**: Your kernel is compiled in native 64-bit mode. This is highly critical, as **ARM32 is actively being deprecated** by upstream container maintainers [176]. Modern Portainer and JDownloader images have dropped or severely restricted ARM32 support [176, 657]. Running this ARM64 kernel ensures full, future-proof software security and updates.

#### 2. Container Isolation (Namespaces & Seccomp Security)
* **Kernel Flags**: `CONFIG_NAMESPACES=y`, `CONFIG_USER_NS=y`, `CONFIG_SECCOMP=y`, `CONFIG_SECCOMP_FILTER=y` [217, 236].
* **Optimization Match**: This ensures that your system natively supports multi-tenant namespace isolation [217] and secure computing filters [236]. Docker leverages this configuration to isolate the process and network spaces of JDownloader, File Browser, and Homer from each other, restricting root system-call hijacking.

#### 3. Resource Allocation and Throttle Controls (Control Groups)
* **Kernel Flags**: `CONFIG_CGROUPS=y`, `CONFIG_MEMCG=y` (Memory Controller) [215, 216].
* **Optimization Match**: Without `CONFIG_MEMCG=y`, Docker would trigger the warning: *"WARNING: No memory limit support"*. Because your kernel explicitly compiles the memory cgroup controller, Portainer can perfectly monitor, graph, and throttle resource bounds (Memory/CPU limits) on a per-container basis.

#### 4. Native Container Networking (veth Bridges)
* **Kernel Flags**: `CONFIG_NETFILTER=y`, `CONFIG_BRIDGE=m`, `CONFIG_VETH=m` [251, 257, 268, 317].
* **Optimization Match**: The core networking bridge (`bridge`) [268] and virtual Ethernet pair (`veth`) [317] drivers are compiled as loadable modules [268, 317]. On Docker engine initialization, the kernel dynamically loads these modules to construct virtual network switches, establishing high-speed container-to-host and container-to-container LAN routes.

#### 5. Compressed swap-in-RAM Optimization (zram)
* **Kernel Flags**: `CONFIG_ZRAM=m` (Compressed block device), `CONFIG_ZSWAP=y` [245, 298].
* **Optimization Match**: This is the ultimate lifespan protector. In `dietpi.txt`, we configured:
  ```ini
  AUTO_SETUP_SWAPFILE_SIZE=1
  AUTO_SETUP_SWAPFILE_LOCATION=zram
  ```
  Since your kernel natively supports zram block compression [298], DietPi will build a compressed, virtual swap area inside your RAM on first boot [298]. This allows the OS to handle heavy memory bursts from your container stacks **without writing a physical swapfile to your SD card or USB SSD**, preserving your storage drive's physical flash cells from write degradation!

---

## Phase 2: Custom Post-Setup Automation Script

DietPi can execute a custom bash script immediately after the automated first-boot installation completes [642]. We can use this hook (`Automation_Custom_Script.sh`) to automatically prepare directory paths, generate configuration files, and deploy the **Homer Dashboard** container [165, 642].

1. Create a new text file on the boot partition of your drive.
2. Name the file exactly **`Automation_Custom_Script.sh`** [642].
3. Paste the following bash code into the file and save it:

```bash
#!/bin/bash
# DietPi First-Boot Custom Post-Setup Script
# This runs automatically once initial updates and package installs finish! [642]

echo "Starting automated homelab directory provisioning and configurations..."

# 1. Create directory paths on the host for Homer, JDownloader, and File Browser config assets [165]
mkdir -p /mnt/dietpi_userdata/jdownloader/config
mkdir -p /mnt/dietpi_userdata/downloads
mkdir -p /mnt/dietpi_userdata/filebrowser

# Make sure the native Homer assets directory exists (installed via software ID 205) [165]
mkdir -p /var/www/homer/assets

# 2. Write out a custom config.yml configuration file for Homer [164]
cat << 'EOF' > /var/www/homer/assets/config.yml
title: "DietPi Hosted Homelab"
subtitle: "Raspberry Pi Portal"
logo: "logo.png"

# Header Banner Message
message:
  title: "Welcome to your DietPi Server!"
  content: "Use the portal links below to manage host containers and services."

# Custom Link Groups
services:
  - name: "Infrastructure"
    icon: "fa-server"
    items:
      - name: "Portainer"
        logo: "assets/tools/portainer.png"
        subtitle: "Container Management Engine"
        url: "http://192.168.0.100:9000"
        target: "_blank"

  - name: "Hosted Services (Native)"
    icon: "fa-server"
    items:
      - name: "Jellyfin"
        logo: "assets/tools/jellyfin.png"
        subtitle: "Native Media Server"
        url: "http://192.168.0.100:8096"
        target: "_blank"
      - name: "File Browser"
        logo: "assets/tools/filebrowser.png"
        subtitle: "Native Web File Explorer"
        url: "http://192.168.0.100:8081"
        target: "_blank"

  - name: "Containerized Services"
    icon: "fa-cloud"
    items:
      - name: "JDownloader"
        logo: "assets/tools/jdownloader.png"
        subtitle: "Dockerized Download Manager"
        url: "https://my.jdownloader.org"
        target: "_blank"
EOF

# 3. Ensure proper directory user permissions [165]
# Native Homer runs under Nginx's www-data user
chown -R www-data:www-data /var/www/homer

# Native File Browser and containerized user storage maps to 'dietpi' user
chown -R dietpi:dietpi /mnt/dietpi_userdata/downloads
chown -R dietpi:dietpi /mnt/dietpi_userdata/jdownloader
chown -R dietpi:dietpi /mnt/dietpi_userdata/filebrowser

# 4. Run the JDownloader Container natively via Docker [165]
# Since dietpi-software installed Docker during first-boot, it is ready to run [638, 642]!
docker run -d \
  --name jdownloader \
  --init \
  --restart=unless-stopped \
  -v /mnt/dietpi_userdata/downloads:/opt/JDownloader/Downloads \
  -v /mnt/dietpi_userdata/jdownloader/config:/opt/JDownloader/app/cfg \
  -p 3129:3129 \
  jaymoulin/jdownloader:latest

# 5. Restart host webserver to apply the new Homer dashboard assets [165]
systemctl restart nginx 2>/dev/null || systemctl restart lighttpd 2>/dev/null

echo "Automated setup completed successfully!"
```

